UPDATE artists SET bio_en = 'José Bello —full name José Manuel Bello Suazo, born in Santo Domingo on 6 April 1953, died in Jacksonville, Florida, on 22 June 2026— was a Dominican salsa singer, composer and bandleader nicknamed «El Magnate de la Salsa», who built his career almost entirely within New York’s Puerto Rican-led salsa scene.

**A stand-in in Queens**

He emigrated to New York with his mother at seventeen, in 1970, with no formal musical training beyond a brief and unsuccessful stint at the conservatory. His neighbor, bassist Carlos Castillo — a veteran of the orchestras of Machito, Tito Rodríguez and Joe Cuba — ran a rehearsal workshop that Bello began tagging along to, until the day its regular singer failed to show up and Bello filled in, impressing everyone present. The two went on to form the «Latin Sextet», a group that played weddings and small parties but never recorded.

**Charangas and a first recording**

His actual recording debut came earlier, on the late composer Julio Gutiérrez’s album «Close Encounters of the Latin Kind», singing «Baila Que Baila». Through his distant cousin, the pianist and arranger Frank Bello, he then joined the charanga group «Charanga América» in the mid-1970s, recording several albums that included the hits «Mañana» and «Se Quema la Sazón».

**«José Bello y su Orquesta»**

In 1980 he formed his own orchestra, partnering with Ray Castro, owner of Lo Mejor Records and the group Conjunto Clásico. His debut LP was a near-total hit, yielding «Guerrera», «Vaselina» and «Basta Ya»; a second, «La Palabra Amigo» (1983), was led by its title track; and a third, gold-pressed «Expresa e Interpreta Sus Sentimientos» (1984), produced the hit «La Esquina Caliente» shortly before the label folded.

**«Canción a un Individuo» and a bond with Colombia**

In the mid-1990s he wrote «Canción a un Individuo», inspired by the anonymous street dealers he observed near the Manhattan club El Corso, composing it in the vocal style of Héctor Lavoe in the hope that the ailing singer might record it. Bello also built a lasting bond with Colombia, especially Cali, where he recorded and toured extensively over the following decades and where, ultimately, his funeral would be held.

**Legacy**

Days before 11 September 2001, Bello performed at one of the World Trade Center’s «Viernes de Salsa» concerts, dedicating «Basta Ya» to a world he described as threatened by nuclear weapons and corruption. He also played the preview party for a Rolling Stones film for a Hollywood crowd in the mid-1980s. Married to the bolero singer and designer Patricia González, he died after a fall in October 2025 injured his spine, having written on social media at the time: «No es un adiós, es un hasta luego, porque yo nací pa’ guarachar».' WHERE slug = 'jose-bello';

